aps, but must not dump facts. We can compare.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate A: quick correct decisions, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, puts concern for others, excellent attention skills. Candidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, moody, loner.\n\nWe need to decide our vote. Let's evaluate: Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone. Candidate C: anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership, unfriendly, eats unhealthily, limited willingness to accept criticism, disorganized. Candidate A: reliability, quick decisions, attention, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. Candidate D: problem solving, concentration, responsibility, but moody, loner, maybe poor crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to weigh criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability/feedback.\n\nCandidate B: strong on reliability (conscientious), stress resilience, technical (weather assessment, computer), but communication issues (pretentious, wrong tone) could affect crew cooperation. No explicit note on decision quality depth, but likely okay.\n\nCandidate C: strong technical competence, anticipation, leadership, but poor feedback orientation, disorganized (reliability), unfriendly (crew cooperation). Also eats unhealthily (maybe irrelevant). So concerns on reliability and crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate A: strong reliability (attention, quick decisions, concern for others), but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education (adaptability). Crew cooperation maybe okay? Difficulty communicating ideas could affect crew.\n\nCandidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but moody, loner (crew cooperation), arrogance? Actually note says \"loner\" and \"moody\". Could affect crew.\n\nThus B seems balanced: strong reliability, stress, technical, but communication issues. A has reliability but communication issues too. C has technical but reliability/feedback issues. D has responsibility but crew issues.\n\nWhich is best? Might be B, as stress resilience and conscientiousness are crucial for long-distance. Communication issues could be mitigated with training. Also B's technical weather assessment is important for long flights.\n\nThus I might shift vote to Candidate B, but need to justify with facts and reference prior discussion. Also we could bring up Candidate A's communication difficulty as a comparable issue, showing B's issues may be less severe? Or note that B's stress handling is proven, while A's stress unknown.\n\nWe could also ask for missing info: stress resilience for A and D, crew cooperation for B and C, decision quality for C. But we can also make argument now.\n\nWe need to produce public message and metadata JSON with vote.\n\nLet's craft: \"I agree with Agent 2 that Candidate B's strengths in reliability and stress resilience outweigh C's technical edge given C's feedback and organization concerns. Additionally, compared to Candidate A's communication difficulty and lack of learning, B's issues are more about tone, which can be coached. Therefore I shift my provisional vote to Candidate B.\"\n\nWe should reference explicit facts: B is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, pretentious, sometimes adopts wrong tone. C's limited willingness to accept criticism, unorganized. A's difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education. D's moody, loner.\n\nWe can also note that we still need to discuss D and A, but for now B seems best.\n\nThus vote B.\n\nNow produce output."}
